* so vaddr and bias stuff is currently borked when pointer encoding
  (e.g. for LSDA) is absolute (e.g. u4 without pcrel). Keep testing
  with pic/pie for now.

* ret locations should be checked, perhaps, to have RSP at CFA-8 and
  all callee-saved regs restored. etc.

* one type of indirect jumps: jmp-ing via c++ vtable. One way to
  detect is state "compatible" with tail-call (Entry state).

* _start seems to be always wrong. Bad unwind info. Report upstream?

* for exception pads at least when they land in our FDE check that CFI
  state matches CFI state for the range of instructions of "call-site".

* switch calls for PIE/PIC look pretty much like this:
1a703:       48 8d 3d f6 b2 fe ff    lea    -0x14d0a(%rip),%rdi        # 5a00 <__abi_tag+0x5704>
1a70a:       48 63 04 87             movslq (%rdi,%rax,4),%rax
1a70e:       48 01 f8                add    %rdi,%rax
1a711:       ff e0                   jmp    *%rax

So adding known-constant and (loaded with sign-extension from possible
switch table) could be simple-ish AbsVal extensions and get us some
switch table handling. Different indexes we could either handle by
trying to track ranges (e.g. %rax above) or just scanning from 0 until
table is referring to "our" FDE.

-----


Quick impressions, from the histograms I already collected — unverified beyond that.

The big four, roughly in order of how much volume they'd buy back:

1. Cross-FDE jumps — unconditional jump out of FDE with rsp at CFA+N
(should be CFA-8 for a tail call). 323 in libstdc++, 94 in gcc, 56 in
libc. This is the biggest thing after jump tables and I suspect it's
the cheapest real win. It's mostly .cold fragments and throw/noreturn
helpers that live in their own FDE: the jump is legitimate, our state
is mid-frame, and the check demands a tail-call exit state it was
never going to have. The fix looks like the same machinery §6 of the
plan already needs — if the target is the start of (or inside) another
FDE, compare our state against that FDE's row at that address instead
of demanding CFA-8. The sibling category tail call in an FDE that did
not start at a canonical function entry (128 in gcc, 20 in libstdc++)
is the same story from the other end.

2. CFI says rN is saved at [CFA-N], but we cannot say what is stored
there — 389+ in libstdc++, the single largest category there and the
reason libstdc++'s number is so ugly. My guess is this is mostly the
at_function_entry=false seeding path: an FDE fragment seeds
unmentioned registers to kTop, so every slot the CFI names comes back
unknowable. If that's right it's a seeding/precision question, not a
semantics one. Worth confirming before anyone writes code, because the
alternative explanation (spills through unmodelled instructions) needs
a completely different fix.

3. Realignment and DWARF expressions, concentrated in libcrypto — ~170
return with untracked callee-saved register, 31 CFA declared as rsp+N
but rsp holds unknown, 36 CFA is given by a DWARF expression. This is
the hand-written perlasm plus and $-16,%rsp cluster, and it's exactly
what the DRAP recogniser + tiny expression interpreter in AGENT.md's
gap list is for. Bigger job, and it's one binary's problem more than a
general one.

4. Landing pads are much less of the story than AGENT.md says —
lsda-reader.cc clearly did its job. What's left in the coverage-gap
bucket is dominated by jump tables.

Two oddities worth a look at some point: 11 cannot decode the
instruction at this address in libcrypto (Capstone gaps or actual data
in .text — either way, currently invisible), and libc's handful of CFA
is declared as rdi+N, but rdi holds entry rdi, which smells like
setcontext/swapcontext and is probably a true "this is genuinely
weird" review rather than a gap.

If I had to pick one thing to do after switch tables, it'd be #1 —
it's a single structural idea, it's the top category in two of the
five binaries, and it reuses work the jump-table change already forces
you to do.

✻ Churned for 35s

❯ Also real quick without much thinking, give me a sketch of how #1
could be done. I have my thoughts too,but prefer to see yours. Again,
just a sketch. Give me answer "off the top of your head", brain-storm
style (don't worry to be wrong)

Off the top of my head, brainstorm quality:

The core move: stop asking "is this a valid tail call?" and start
asking "does the state I'm handing over agree with what the target's
own FDE assumes?" That's the same state-vs-row comparison
RowChecker::Check already does — nothing in it cares which FDE the row
came from. So look up the target PC's FDE, get its row at that
address, and run the existing check against our current state.

Why this is compositional and doesn't blow up: we never walk into the
other FDE. It already gets its own independent walk, seeded by
SeedFromRow(..., at_function_entry=false). That seed is currently
trusted unconditionally — that's the whole reason SeedFromRow
exists. This change turns the assumption into a verified fact by
checking it against every actual predecessor edge. So it's strictly
stronger than today, and it costs one row lookup per cross-FDE edge,
not a re-analysis.

The load-bearing assumption: it only works because CFA is the same
symbolic quantity in both FDEs. That's true for .cold fragments of one
function — their CFI is written against the same canonical frame
address, which is exactly what makes unwinding through them work — and
false for a real tail call to a different function, where the callee's
CFA is a different thing entirely and the slot offsets aren't
comparable.

Distinguishing the two without a naming convention (same shape as
FallThroughIsReal's two-condition test): try the exit-state check
first; if the state is a valid tail-call exit, bless and stop. If it
isn't, then it can't be a tail call — a tail call with a
half-torn-down frame is broken code — so the shared-CFA reading is the
only one left, and the row comparison applies. Only if both fail do
you report. No list of cold-fragment names, no .cold suffix matching.

Plumbing: FDEChecker is one-FDE-at-a-time today, so it needs a global
"row at any PC" service. Sorted (pc_begin, pc_end) index built during
EnumerateFDEs — unwind-check.cc already builds something like that for
the overlap check, so reuse it — plus a lazily-decoded, cached
CFITable per FDE. Binary search, decode on miss.

Severity: ship the disagreement as REVIEW with its own distinct
message, measure, then decide about promoting. It's arguably a true
MISMATCH (two FDEs of one function disagreeing about the frame at a
transfer between them is a real defect), but it's a new check resting
on a new assumption, so let it earn it.

Freebies if it works: the jump-table out-of-FDE targets from the
plan's §6 stop needing special handling, and tail call in an FDE that
did not start at a canonical function entry (128 in gcc) is probably
the same edge seen from the other side.

Where I'd expect it to be wrong: the false-bless case where a
genuinely broken tail call happens to match the target's row — I'd
guess rare, and it's the _dl_runtime_resolve shape. And I'm not
certain the shared-CFA claim holds for every cross-FDE jump; ICF'd
shared blocks like that libstdc++ throw target at 0xb98fa are reached
from multiple unrelated functions, so their CFI can't be relative to
any one caller's CFA. Those probably need to fall out as reviews
rather than be checked — worth looking at before committing to the
design.
